> I'm interested what the _actual_ justification for omitting the "i" in
> aluminium is, which appears in every language i'm aware of _except for
> U.S. english_.

The person who named it, Sir Humphry Davy, originally called it
"alumium", then "aluminum".  The British took it upon themselves 4 years
later to amend that to "aluminium".  So really, the American spelling
doesn't omit the 'i', since the 'i' wasn't there in the first place.  
Rather, the British spelling _adds_ the 'i'.

As to why Davy did it that way, I don't know.  He named several other
elements as well, such as sodium.  

> "Aluminium" is derived from "alumen", and just like in "Sodium"
> (derived from "soda"[1]) and "Potassium" (derived from "potash"), an
> "i" is placed between the "stem" word and the "-um". It looks rather
> arbitrary to me, but "aluminum" is no less arbitrary.

I think that's exactly it.  It's arbitrary.  When Davy was naming
elements, he though that "aluminum" sounded better than "alumium" or
"aluminium" and that "sodium" sounded better than "sodum".  

